  "worked_solution": "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},\u2026,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{\u2015}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{\u2015}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{\u2015}{x}.$ There are $n=12$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=-60,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\u2015}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-60) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2}=440,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 440 \\\\  & =\\frac{440}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{\u2015}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{\u2015}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}-{(-5)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{110}{3}-25 \\\\  & =\\frac{110-75}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
